WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.340
A counting method is correct when every outcome has

00:00:03.340 --> 00:00:04.750
exactly one address.

00:00:05.380 --> 00:00:07.820
From the outcome you can tell which choices made it,

00:00:07.820 --> 00:00:10.710
and every set of choices makes a real outcome.

00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:14.060
If both are true, nothing is missed and nothing

00:00:14.060 --> 00:00:15.010
is counted twice.

00:00:16.110 --> 00:00:18.790
Double counting and missed cases are invisible if you

00:00:18.790 --> 00:00:20.280
only look at the final number.

00:00:20.850 --> 00:00:23.010
Checking the addresses is the way to trust a count

00:00:23.010 --> 00:00:24.100
without redoing it.

00:00:24.920 --> 00:00:27.630
Five different students sit in a row of five seats.

00:00:27.920 --> 00:00:29.830
Three are girls and two are boys.

00:00:30.340 --> 00:00:32.630
The two boys must not sit next to each other.

00:00:33.260 --> 00:00:34.890
In how many ways can they be seated?

00:00:35.560 --> 00:00:37.860
Pause and look for a way to build every legal

00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:39.210
row exactly once.

00:00:42.970 --> 00:00:44.160
Seat the girls first.

00:00:44.890 --> 00:00:47.100
Three girls can be arranged in six orders.

00:00:47.770 --> 00:00:50.170
Around them are four gaps: one at each end

00:00:50.170 --> 00:00:51.220
and two between.

00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:54.030
Put each boy into a different gap.

00:00:54.560 --> 00:00:56.830
The first boy has four gaps to choose from,

00:00:57.060 --> 00:00:58.170
and the second has three.

00:00:58.620 --> 00:01:01.000
That is twelve ways, so there are six times

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:02.950
twelve, seventy-two seatings.

00:01:03.780 --> 00:01:04.930
Now check the addresses.

00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:06.610
Take any legal row.

00:01:07.300 --> 00:01:09.210
Remove the boys and you see the girls' order.

00:01:09.660 --> 00:01:11.340
Look where the boys were and you see which

00:01:11.340 --> 00:01:12.310
gaps they used.

00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:15.870
So each legal row comes from exactly one set of choices.

00:01:16.460 --> 00:01:18.530
The count is complete and has no repeats.

00:01:19.610 --> 00:01:21.340
A second method confirms it.

00:01:21.730 --> 00:01:24.580
Five people can sit in one hundred and twenty ways.

00:01:25.030 --> 00:01:28.290
Glue the boys together as one block: four objects in

00:01:28.290 --> 00:01:31.320
twenty-four orders, times two for the boys swapping.

00:01:31.850 --> 00:01:33.660
That is forty-eight unwanted rows.

00:01:34.810 --> 00:01:37.320
One hundred and twenty minus forty-eight is seventy-two.

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:41.110
Ask two questions of any counting method.

00:01:41.620 --> 00:01:44.130
Can I recover the choices from every legal outcome?

00:01:44.540 --> 00:01:46.770
And does every choice give a legal outcome?

00:01:47.480 --> 00:01:50.280
Notebook challenge: in how many ways can four different

00:01:50.280 --> 00:01:53.820
books stand on a shelf if two particular books must not

00:01:53.820 --> 00:01:54.970
be next to each other?
